# Keyboard Familiar

Keyboard Familiar turns a SteelSeries keyboard into a quiet glance deck at the edge of your attention.
Clock and system-pulse cards rotate on a supported screen, an optional focus countdown joins when you need
it, and important events preempt the deck. Alerts can also flash the function-key zone on supported
SteelSeries keyboards, so useful signals do not require an OLED.

The product stays deliberately out of the way. Regular cards never alter keyboard lighting. Missing NVIDIA
telemetry removes one source instead of stopping the deck. A local simulator makes every interaction safe
to explore before SteelSeries hardware is involved.

## What it feels like

With the default configuration, a screen rotates every 15 seconds:

```text
NOW
Sat 10:42 AM

SYSTEM PULSE
CPU 18% · RAM 62%

FOCUS · 21m
Write launch note
```

The focus card appears only while a session is active. A completed focus session or a VRAM threshold alert
preempts the rotation. If `function_key_lighting` is configured, that alert also flashes the function keys
in the chosen color. Commands and scripts can place their own quiet cards or alerts with `familiar show`.

## Requirements

- Windows 10 or 11
- Python 3.12 or newer
- [SteelSeries GG](https://steelseries.com/gg) with Engine running
- At least one configured hardware capability:
  - a GameSense-compatible screen for the glance deck; or
  - a supported SteelSeries keyboard function-key zone for alerts
- An NVIDIA GPU is optional. When available, VRAM telemetry uses `nvidia-ml-py` with `nvidia-smi` fallback.

The adapter follows SteelSeries' official [GameSense event API](https://github.com/SteelSeries/gamesense-sdk/blob/master/doc/api/sending-game-events.md),
[screen handler schema](https://github.com/SteelSeries/gamesense-sdk/blob/master/doc/api/json-handlers-screen.md),
and [color handler schema](https://github.com/SteelSeries/gamesense-sdk/blob/master/doc/api/json-handlers-color.md).

## Install and reach first success

Open PowerShell in the repository root:

```powershell
py -3.12 -m venv .venv
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process Bypass
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"

familiar setup
familiar preview
familiar doctor --simulate
familiar render dry-run
```

`setup` copies the repository configuration to `%APPDATA%\Keyboard Familiar` and uses
`%LOCALAPPDATA%\Keyboard Familiar` for runtime state. After that, commands work from any directory. A
repository-local `config` directory still takes precedence for development; `FAMILIAR_CONFIG_DIR` selects
another location explicitly.

The next three commands teach and validate the complete product without contacting hardware. Then start
SteelSeries GG and verify the configured real capabilities:

```powershell
familiar doctor
familiar show "Hardware OK" --title FAMILIAR
familiar show "Alert path" --title CHECK --alert
```

Observe `Hardware OK` on the physical screen. If `function_key_lighting` is configured, observe the alert
color on the function keys for `Alert path`. Alert lighting uses a separate, heartbeat-free GameSense
channel so Engine releases the function keys back to their normal lighting after its idle timeout.
GameSense reports whether Engine accepted the handlers, but it
does not enumerate matching physical devices or acknowledge per-device rendering; observation is the final
hardware check.

Start ordinary operation:

```powershell
familiar run
```

Leave it running in a terminal or launch it with your preferred Windows startup mechanism. `Ctrl+C` stops
it cleanly.

## Everyday commands

```powershell
# See what will rotate, without touching hardware
familiar preview

# Start a countdown; omit --minutes to use deck.yaml's default
familiar focus start "Review pull request" --minutes 25
familiar focus status
familiar focus stop

# Put a quiet card on the screen
familiar show "Build passed" --title SHIP

# Preempt the deck and signal configured function-key lighting
familiar show "Deploy needs attention" --title DEPLOY --alert

# Return later without relearning the current state
familiar status

# Inspect recent decisions and failures
familiar trace tail --lines 50
```

`show` is intentionally script-friendly. For example, a build script can run `familiar show "Tests failed"
--title BUILD --alert` without learning the internal event model. The low-level `trigger` command remains
available but hidden for compatibility and development.

## Configure the deck to taste

`config/deck.yaml` is the main user-facing customization surface:

```yaml
deck:
  interval_seconds: 15
  cards:
    - source: clock
      title: NOW
      time_format: "%a %I:%M %p"
    - source: system
      title: SYSTEM PULSE
    - source: focus
      title: FOCUS

focus:
  default_minutes: 25
```

Cards appear in list order. Disable a card with `enabled: false`; `focus` automatically disappears when no
session is active. Supported built-in sources are `clock`, `system`, and `focus`. Titles are limited to 20
characters because small hardware screens reward concise language. `familiar preview` validates changes
and shows the resulting rotation immediately.

The clock uses `runtime.timezone` from `config/app.yaml`. Use `local` for the Windows system timezone or an
IANA name such as `America/Chicago` when a fixed timezone is intentional.

## Configure SteelSeries capabilities

`config/plugins.yaml` describes the hardware behavior honestly:

```yaml
plugins:
  glance_deck:
    enabled: true
  gpu_vram:
    enabled: true
    every_seconds: 3
    gpu_index: 0
    change_threshold_pct: 2.0
    alert_threshold_pct: 75.0
  steelseries:
    enabled: true
    mode: gamesense
    capabilities: [screen, function_key_lighting]
    alert_color: [255, 64, 32]
    timeout_seconds: 2.0
```

Supported capabilities are:

- `screen` — enables quiet cards and alert text on generic GameSense screened devices.
- `function_key_lighting` — flashes the keyboard function-key zone for alert events only.

For an alert-only keyboard without a screen, set `capabilities: [function_key_lighting]`. Quiet `show`
commands then fail with an explanation instead of pretending something was displayed; `show --alert`
continues to work. Set `mode: simulate` only for an explicitly simulated configuration. Command-level
`--simulate` overrides production mode for safe experiments.

The previous `steelseries_oled` block remains accepted as a compatibility alias and implies `screen` only.

## Diagnosis and recovery

`familiar doctor` treats the product as a set of cooperating capabilities:

- validates every live configuration value;
- samples all glance providers while isolating a broken provider from healthy ones;
- reports optional NVIDIA telemetry as available or degraded;
- validates focus state and gives the reset command if it is malformed;
- registers the configured GameSense screen and lighting handlers;
- states the boundary between accepted Engine handlers and unobservable physical-device delivery.

Common recovery paths:

- **SteelSeries discovery file missing** — start GG, open Engine, wait a few seconds, and rerun `doctor`.
- **GG restarted or changed ports** — the next card automatically rediscovers Engine. Use `doctor` if it
  remains unavailable.
- **No NVIDIA telemetry** — the clock, system pulse, focus, and manual alerts continue. Update the driver
  or disable `gpu_vram` if the warning is not useful.
- **Corrupt focus state** — run `familiar focus stop`. Other deck providers remain active meanwhile.
- **Stale runtime** — `familiar status` calls it out; restart `familiar run` and inspect the trace.
- **Lighting works but no card appears** — confirm `screen` is configured and the attached device has a
  GameSense-compatible screen.
- **Screen works but lighting does not** — confirm `function_key_lighting` is configured and observe an
  alert, not a regular card.

## Simulator and verification boundary

```powershell
familiar preview
familiar show "Quiet check" --simulate
familiar show "Alert check" --alert --simulate
familiar focus start "Simulator session" --minutes 5 --simulate
familiar render dry-run
familiar doctor --simulate
```

The simulator proves provider sampling, deck rotation, focus persistence and completion, event policy,
scene preemption, routing, screen normalization, alert color payloads, console mirroring, runtime state,
and failure propagation. Protocol tests run the real adapter against a deterministic local HTTP Engine and
verify metadata, separate glance/alert bindings, screen handlers, function-key color handlers, events,
keepalive, Engine errors, and endpoint rediscovery.

It cannot prove that a particular model matches SteelSeries' generic handler, that firmware renders it, or
that the result is visually correct on every screen. Use the two real `show` commands in the installation
section and observe the physical device.

## Development checks

```powershell
python -m ruff format --check .
python -m ruff check .
python -m compileall -q familiar tests
python -m pytest -q
python -m build

familiar preview
familiar doctor --simulate
familiar render dry-run
```

CI runs installation, lint, formatting, compilation, tests, package builds, and product-level simulated
smoke flows on Windows and Linux.

## Architecture and extension seam

The modular-monolith flow remains visible: providers produce `GlanceCard` values; the deck selects a quiet
card or pending alert; events update state and become proposals; scene arbitration protects attention; and
the router sends directives to configured surfaces. `CardProvider` is the narrow source seam. New built-in
sources need to return a title, body, and optional alert semantics; they automatically participate in deck
configuration, preview, doctor isolation, simulation, arbitration, persistence, and device rendering.

See [docs/extending.md](docs/extending.md) for the concrete extension path. YAML-defined arbitrary code is
intentionally not supported. The Python rules and scene policy remain authoritative; `config/rules.yaml`
and `config/scenes.yaml` are architecture references retained for compatibility.
